WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.490
Eradication focuses on removing the cause of the incident,

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:05.550
not just quieting the visible symptom.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.260
If containment is about limiting harm now,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.705
eradication is about preventing the same issue from continuing or returning.

00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:14.110
The root cause might be a vulnerable system, an unsafe configuration,

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:15.535
an exposed credential,

00:00:15.535 --> 00:00:19.840
unauthorized access, a malicious artifact, a weak process, or a gap in ownership.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.405
The right fix depends on the analysis.

00:00:22.405 --> 00:00:25.455
Sometimes eradication means patching and validating a system.

00:00:25.455 --> 00:00:28.915
Sometimes it means rebuilding a host, removing unauthorized access,

00:00:28.915 --> 00:00:31.790
correcting cloud permissions, disabling an unsafe setting,

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.840
or changing a process that allowed the problem to happen.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.895
This work should be authorized and tracked because eradication often touches production

00:00:38.895 --> 00:00:39.700
systems.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:40.700
Verification matters:

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.640
the team should confirm that the cause was addressed and that the environment no longer

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.290
shows the same

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:44.885
behavior.

00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:47.325
Incomplete eradication creates recurrence risk.

00:00:47.325 --> 00:00:50.955
A clean-looking dashboard is not enough if the underlying path remains open.

00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:54.430
Good eradication produces evidence of the fix, the validation performed,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.501
and any residual risk that still needs ownership.

00:00:57.501 --> 00:01:01.120
Eradication also depends on knowing when a visible symptom is only a symptom.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.360
Resetting one account may not be enough if the access path remains open.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.080
Removing one artifact may not be enough if the vulnerable configuration remains.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.165
The response team should look for the condition that allowed the incident to happen or

00:01:11.165 --> 00:01:11.670
continue.
